<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-25" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Introduction to 4-momentum</title>

  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise>
    <p>
        If the textbook didn't convince you, we will go through a <q>derivation</q> 
        of what a relativistic momentum should look like. Let's propose the 
        following form for the momentum:
        <m>
            ~{\vec{p}=\alpha(|\vec{v}|)m\vec{v}}
        </m>. This follows the idea of <q>proportional to mass</q> and
         <q>points along the particle trajectory</q>. Explain why it must be true 
        that the limit of
        <m>
            \alpha(v)
        </m>
        when
        <m>
            v \ll c
        </m>
        (or
        <m>
            v \ll 1
        </m>
        in SR units) must be 1.
    </p>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        Now let's consider the symmetric collision shown below.
        Two particles of mass <m>m</m> with opposite velocities hit each other in a
        way that their y velocities reverse during after the collision.
        In this frame, the total momentum is 0.
        We have also defined events ''A, B, C, D'' which could be thought of as the
        balls being ''thrown'' (A and C) and ''caught'' later on (B and D).
    </p>

    <image width="80%" source="images\activities\lec25\collisionsketch.png"/>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                In the space below, draw the collision as observed by someone moving to the right at speed
                <m>
                    u_{x}
                </m>
                (frame S) and, next to that drawing, draw the collision as observed by someone moving to the left at speed
                <m>
                    u_{x}
                </m>
                (frame S'). Identify the events ABCD in both diagrams. To fix the notation, call the velocities of the top and bottom particles (in S)
                <m>
                    \vec{w}
                </m>
                and
                <m>
                    \vec{v}
                </m>
                , respectively.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                From your pictures, do the x components of the velocities change in either S or S'? What about the y components?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
               In terms of the times
                <m>
                    \Delta t_{AB}, \Delta t_{CD}
                </m>
                , the distance
                <m>
                    d
                </m>
                , and
                <m>
                    m
                </m>
                , find expressions for
                <m>
                    \Delta p_{y,A}
                </m>
                and
                <m>
                    \Delta p_{y,B}
                </m>
                (I'll do that on the board). Which of these times is a proper time?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Explain why <m>\Delta t_{AB} = \Delta t'_{CD}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Using the invariance of the interval, a Lorentz transformation, or whatever argument you want, show that 
                <m>\Delta t_{CD} = \gamma (v_x) \Delta t_{AB}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        This is a calculator exercise. Consider trying to get yourself to 
        <m>v=0.8</m> relative to Earth so you can do interstellar travel 
        using time dilation. How much energy would that require? 
        In MO, 1kWh from the power company (<m>3.6\times 10^6</m> J) costs 15 
        cents. If you could get the energy at that price, how much would that 
        cost? Give your answer in billions (<m>10^9</m>) of dollars.
    </p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <p>
        At what speed do you have to be moving for the Newtonian expression for
        the momentum to be off by 1%? What about the kinetic energy? 
        Desmos/Wolfram can help for the 2nd one.
    </p>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
